2025-09-13 17:06:41 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - [Request b952b3e4-e4fe-4b7d-a8dc-21329c866871] JSON parsing successful: {'text': 'Certified questions are questions of law that this court may answer when a resolution of that question is necessary to resolve a case before the federal court. RCW 2.60.020; Convoyant, LLC v. DeepThink, LLC, 200 Wn.2d 72, 73, 514 P.3d 643 (2022). Certified questions are questions of law we review de novo. Carlson v. Glob. Client Sols., LLC, 171 Wn.2d 486, 493, 256 P.3d 321 (2011). We also review the meaning of a statute de novo. Dep\'t of Ecology v. Campbell & Gwinn, LLC, 146 Wn.2d 1, 9, 43 P.3d 4 (2003). We have long held that pro se litigants are bound by the same rules of procedure and substantive law as licensed attorneys. Holder v. City of Vancouver, 136 Wn. App. 104, 106, 147 P.3d 641 (2006); In re Marriage of Olson, 69 Wn. App. 621, 626, 850 P.2d 527 (1993) (noting courts are "under no obligation to grant special favors to . . . a pro se litigant."). Thus, a pro se appellant\'s failure to "identify any specific legal issues . . . cite any authority" or comply with procedural rules may still preclude appellate review. State v. Marintorres, 93 Wn. App. 442, 452, 969 P.2d 501 (1999). Statutory interpretation is also an issue of law we review de novo. Spokane County v. Dep\'t of Fish & Wildlife, 192 Wn.2d 453, 457, 430 P.3d 655 (2018).', 'type': 'text'}
